<%# Laser math's tube reading (Laser::Tube): the number, how it is read, each stock on its own, and what it cannot say. %>
<% t = @tube %>
<% large = LaserJob::MACHINES[Laser::Tube::YARDSTICK] %>
<% name = LaserJob::MACHINES[@machine] %>
<% w = ->(v) { "#{laser_number(v)} W" } %>
<% more = ((1 / t.speed - 1) * 100).round %>
<section class="card laser-tube-card" id="tube" aria-labelledby="tube-title">
  <div class="card-head">
    <h2 id="tube-title">Tube reading</h2>
    <%= render ChipComponent.new("Log through #{laser_day(t.through)}", tone: :info) %>
  </div>
  <% if t.own? %>
    <p class="laser-tube-lead">Rated <%= w.(t.rated) %>. Over its last <%= Laser::Tube::WINDOW %> months, going by the log, this laser cuts like about <strong><%= w.(t.watts) %></strong> against its own average. The reading updates every night.</p>
  <% else %>
    <p class="laser-tube-lead">Rated <%= w.(t.rated) %>. Based on the log, this laser cuts like about <strong><%= w.(t.watts) %></strong>, and the reading updates every night.</p>
  <% end %>
  <div class="tiles">
    <%= render StatTileComponent.new(value: w.(t.watts), label: "Cuts like", sub: "likely #{laser_number(t.low)} to #{w.(t.high)}", tone: :headline) %>
    <%= render StatTileComponent.new(value: w.(t.rated), label: "Rated") %>
    <% if t.own? %>
      <%= render StatTileComponent.new(value: "#{"+" if more.positive?}#{more}%", label: "Energy for the same cut", sub: "against its average") %>
      <%= render StatTileComponent.new(value: number_with_delimiter(t.people), label: "People", sub: "cut on it in the last #{Laser::Tube::WINDOW} months") %>
    <% else %>
      <%= render StatTileComponent.new(value: "#{laser_number(t.speed * 100)}%", label: "Speed from the #{large.split.first}", sub: "at the same power") %>
      <%= render StatTileComponent.new(value: number_with_delimiter(t.people), label: "People", sub: "cut the same stock on both lasers") %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
  <%= laser_tube_gauge(t) %>

  <h3>How it is read</h3>
  <% if t.own? %>
    <p>This laser has nothing to be read against, so it is read against itself. Section 2's law is fitted to all its clean cuts since <%= laser_day(t.since) %>: the energy people used for each material and thickness. From <%= laser_day(t.from) %> to <%= laser_day(t.through) %>, <%= t.people %> people logged clean cuts on it; each person's cuts are compared with the law, one vote per person. The middle person used <%= more.abs %>% <%= more.negative? ? "less" : "more" %> energy than the law. Counting the law, the average of the whole log, as its rated <%= w.(t.rated) %>, it cuts like <%= w.(t.watts) %> now. The range comes from resampling those people.</p>
    <p><%= laser_tube_advice(t) %> The energy index by quarter, at the bottom of this page, shows the same thing over the whole log.</p>
  <% else %>
    <p>Since <%= laser_day(t.since) %>, <%= t.people %> people cut the same material and thickness on both lasers and logged it as working. For each person, compare power &times; passes &divide; speed on the two machines. The middle person used about <%= format("%.2f", 1 / t.speed) %> times as much on the <%= name %> as on the <%= large %>. Counting the <%= large %> at its rated <%= w.(t.against) %>, that is a <%= w.(t.watts) %> laser. The range comes from resampling those people.</p>
    <% if (ex = t.example) %>
      <p>For example, the most used setting for <%= laser_number(ex[:mm], 1) %> mm <%= ex[:material].downcase %> is <strong><%= laser_setting(*ex[:large]) %></strong> on the <%= large %> and <strong><%= laser_setting(*ex[:mini]) %></strong> on the <%= name %>. To bring a setting over, keep the power and run at <%= laser_share(t.speed) %> the speed. If Find a setting has jobs for your stock, use those first.</p>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
  <% if t.stocks.any? %>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption class="muted small"><%= t.own? ? "Each stock read on its own, from the people who cut it in the last #{Laser::Tube::WINDOW} months." : "Each stock read on its own, from the people who cut it on both lasers." %> A weaker beam shows as the same gap on every stock; habit shows as stocks that disagree.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Stock</th><th class="num"><%= t.own? ? "People" : "People on both" %></th><th class="num">Reads as</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <% t.stocks.each do |s| %>
            <tr><td><%= s[:material] %>, <%= laser_number(s[:mm], 1) %> mm</td><td class="num"><%= s[:people] %></td><td class="num"><%= w.(s[:watts]) %></td></tr>
          <% end %>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <h3>What it cannot say</h3>
  <ul class="laser-tube-limits">
    <% if t.own? %>
      <li>It compares this laser with itself. <%= w.(t.rated) %> means as strong as its average since <%= t.since.strftime("%B %Y") %>, not a measured <%= w.(t.rated) %>. A tube that was already weak then reads as <%= w.(t.rated) %>.</li>
      <li>People change habits. When the class settings change, the reading moves with them: the jump in spring 2023 under the energy index was a new taught setting, not the tube.</li>
      <li>It is the yardstick for the <%= LaserJob::MACHINES["mini"] %>'s reading, which counts this laser at its rated <%= w.(t.rated) %> over the same whole log.</li>
    <% else %>
      <li>It takes the <%= large %> at its rated <%= w.(t.against) %>. If that beam is weak, this one reads high, and the other way round. The <%= link_to "#{large}'s own reading", laser_path(view: "math", anchor: "tube") %> shows how it is doing.</li>
      <li>It reads what people chose. People copy the class settings, so habit is in it as well as power.</li>
      <li>The rest of this page keeps the rated <%= w.(t.rated) %>, so its J/mm read about <%= laser_number((t.rated / t.watts - 1) * 100) %>% high next to the <%= large %>'s.</li>
    <% end %>
    <li>It reads the whole beam: tube, mirrors, lens and power supply. It cannot say which part is down.</li>
    <li>When a tube is replaced, the reading starts over from that day. A beam power meter, or the same test grid cut on both lasers, would measure it directly.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
